   202	*Proof.*
   203	- (i) Integrality: a − bα ∈ 𝔞. For the norm: f is primitive, so Gauss's content lemma for Dedekind domains (over the
   204	  Galois closure, content(f) = (t)·∏_σ(1, σα)) gives N(𝔞) = |t|^{−1}. Then N(𝔍_P) = |N_{K/Q}(a − bα)|·|t| =
   205	  |F(a, b)|.
   206	- (ii) For r ∤ t, α is r-integral and 𝔞 is trivial at r. For r ∤ t·disc f, Z_(r)[α] is the r-maximal order and Dedekind–Kummer applies. If r | F(a, b)
   207	  then r ∤ b (otherwise r | ta^d, so r | t), so a − bα lies only in (r, α − ab^{−1}), with valuation
   208	  v_r(N(a − bα)) = v_r(F(a, b)).
   209	- (iii) Buchmann–Lenstra (1994) / Cohen GTM 138 Alg. 6.1.8 (Round 2 at a known prime), 6.2.9 (decomposition),
   210	  4.8.17 (valuations). Then v_𝔯(𝔍_P) = v_𝔯(z_P) − min(v_𝔯(t), v_𝔯(β)), with z_P = ta − bβ ∈ Z[β] and
   211	  t𝔞 = (t, β). Finding the bad r: compute t·disc(f) mod r for r ≤ B, in Õ(B) time. ∎
   212	
   213	**Columns.** One column per rational prime q ≤ B (entry e_q), one column "g₁" (entry E_i), and one column per prime
   214	ideal 𝔯 above r ≤ B that occurs (entry Σ_ℓ e_ℓ v_𝔯(𝔍_{P_ℓ})). Hence C_col ≤ (d + 1)π(B) + 1. Signs are not columns.
   215	Let E ∈ Z^{M×C_col} be the exact exponent matrix and
   216	  Λ₁ := {x ∈ Z^M : xᵀE = 0},  γ_x := ∏_P (a_P − b_Pα)^{y_P(x)},  y_P(x) := Σ_i x_i Σ_{ℓ: P_ℓ = P} e_{iℓ}.
   217	(Atoms are cached per (A, ℓ), so P is a function of ℓ.)
   218	
   219	**Lemma XK1 (exactness ⇒ units).** For x ∈ Λ₁, γ_x ∈ O_K^×.
   220	- *Proof.* (γ_x) = ∏_P 𝔍_P^{y_P}·𝔞^{Σ_P y_P}.
   221	  - Σ_P y_P = Σ_i x_i E_i = 0 (the g₁ column).
   222	  - For every 𝔯 above r ≤ B, v_𝔯 = Σ_i x_i E_{i,𝔯} = 0.
   223	  - Every prime dividing some 𝔍_P lies above some r ≤ B, because N(𝔍_P) = |F(P)| is B-smooth, and every such 𝔯 is a
   224	    column.
   225	  - So (γ_x) = O_K. ∎
   226	
   227	**Lemma XK2 (the reduction map).** Let 𝔭 be the prime of O_K above p with α ≡ m. It exists and is unique of degree 1,
   228	because p ∤ t and m is a simple root mod p (the rejection p | f′(m) of thmA SAMPLE_FIELD). Reduction
   229	red_𝔭 : O_𝔭^× → F_p^× is a homomorphism with red_𝔭(a − bα) = a − bm = ψ(a − bα) ≠ 0. For x ∈ Λ₁:
   230	  W(x) := ∏_i T_i^{x_i} ≡ ±red_𝔭(γ_x)  (mod p).                                          (4.1)
   231	- *Proof.*
   232	  - Each a − bα is a 𝔭-unit. 𝔍_P has norm |F(P)| < p, and v_𝔭(𝔞) = 0 since p ∤ t.
   233	  - Take (2.1) to the power x_i and multiply. The q-, g₁- and sign-factors have exponents xᵀE = 0 in the rational
   234	    columns (the signs give ±1).
   235	  - The ψ-factors give ∏_P ψ(a_P − b_Pα)^{y_P} = red_𝔭(γ_x). ∎
   236	
   237	**Lemma XK3 (lattices).**
   238	- Λ₁ is saturated (it is the kernel of an integer matrix), and rank Λ₁ ≥ M − C_col.
   239	- The map u: Λ₁ → O_K^×/μ_K, x ↦ [γ_x], is a homomorphism to a free group of rank r_U := r₁ + r₂ − 1 ≤ d − 1.
   240	- Λ₂ := ker u = {x ∈ Λ₁ : γ_x ∈ μ_K} is saturated in Z^M (if jx ∈ Λ₂ then γ_x^j ∈ μ_K, so γ_x ∈ μ_K), and
   241	  rank Λ₂ ≥ M − C_col − r_U ≥ 1 when M ≥ C_col + d.
   242	- Every primitive vector of Λ₂ is primitive in Z^M. ∎
   243	
   244	**Lemma XK4 (unit elimination; proved).** Given s := r_U + 1 ≤ d independent x₁, …, x_s ∈ Λ₁ with entries of at
   245	most β_x bits, one finds a nonzero n ∈ Z^s with Σ_j n_j x_j ∈ Λ₂ in time poly(d)·(β_x + log p)^{2+o(1)}·M.
   246	
   247	*Proof.*
   248	- **Log vectors.** Let Log: K^× → R^{r₁+r₂}, Log(z) = (log|σ_i z|)_{real} ∪ (2 log|σ_j z|)_{complex pairs}, and
   249	  v_j := Log(γ_{x_j}) = Σ_P y_P(x_j)·Log(a_P − b_Pα). These are logs of units, so they lie in the unit lattice
   250	  Λ_U = Log(O_K^×), of rank r_U.
   251	- **Gap (Blanksby–Montgomery 1971).** A non-torsion algebraic integer of degree d′ ≤ d has Mahler measure
   252	  > 1 + 1/(52d′ log 6d′). For a unit z of K, log M(z) ≤ d·‖Log z‖_∞. So every nonzero w ∈ Λ_U has
   253	  ‖w‖_∞ ≥ β_d := log(1 + 1/(52d log 6d))/d > 0. By Kronecker, Log z = 0 iff z ∈ μ_K.
   254	- **Relations exist and are small.**
   255	  - Rel := {n ∈ Z^s : Σ n_j v_j = 0} has rank ≥ s − r_U ≥ 1.
   256	  - Fix an LLL-reduced basis u_1..u_{r_U} of Λ_U (only for the proof). Its Gram–Schmidt norms are ≥ 2^{−r_U/2}β_d.
   257	  - So the integer coordinates c_{ji} of v_j satisfy |c_{ji}| ≤ 2^{O(d²)}‖v_j‖/β_d.
   258	  - Cramer on an r_U × (r_U + 1) integer matrix gives a nonzero n₀ ∈ Rel with
   259	    ‖n₀‖ ≤ R := d!·(2^{O(d²)} max_j‖v_j‖/β_d)^{d}.
   260	  - ‖v_j‖ ≤ 2^{β_x}·M·2k·max_P‖Log(a_P − b_Pα)‖, and the last factor is O(log p + Σ log(1 + |σα|)) = O_d(log p).
   261	    So log R = O_d(β_x + log p).
   262	- **LLL.** Let b_j := (e_j, ⌊2^κ v_j⌉) ∈ Z^{s+r₁+r₂}, where ⌊2^κ v_j⌉ is any integer vector within 1 of 2^κ v_j in
   263	  each coordinate (computed from approximations).
   264	  - If n ∈ Rel, then ‖Σ n_j b_j‖ ≤ (1 + s√d)‖n‖.
   265	  - If n ∉ Rel, then ‖Σ n_j b_j‖ ≥ 2^κβ_d − s√d‖n‖.
   266	  - LLL's first vector b has ‖b‖ ≤ 2^{(s−1)/2}(1 + s√d)R =: R′, and its n-part has ‖n‖ ≤ R′.
   267	  - Choose 2^κβ_d > (1 + s√d)R′. Then b's n-part lies in Rel, since otherwise ‖b‖ > R′. And n ≠ 0, since n = 0 would
   268	    make b = 0.
   269	  - So κ = O_d(β_x + log p).
   270	- **Precision.**
   271	  - Roots of f to κ + O(β_x + log p) bits: Õ(d²κ) (Schönhage / Pan).
   272	  - Each log|a − bσα| to that precision: Õ(κ).
   273	  - The sums Σ_P y_P·Log(·) over ≤ 2kM atoms: Õ(M(κ + β_x)).
   274	  - LLL in fixed dimension s + r₁ + r₂ ≤ 2d with O(κ)-bit entries: Õ(κ²).
   275	- **Certification.** In the algorithm, accept n only if the ball |Σ n_j v_j|_∞ < β_d/2 is certified. A true relation
   276	  passes, by the precision. A non-relation cannot pass, by the gap. ∎
   277	
   278	**Lemma XK5 (torsion ⇒ known logarithm).** For x ∈ Λ₂, W(x) ∈ μ_{2w}(F_p), where w := |μ_K|. For d odd, μ_K = {±1},
   279	so W(x) = ±1. Then λ := log_g W(x) ∈ {0, n/2} is read off. In general λ = j·n/e with e = gcd(2w, n), and j < e is
   280	found by trying.
   281	- *Proof.* By (4.1), W(x) = ±red_𝔭(ζ) with ζ ∈ μ_K, and red_𝔭 maps μ_K into μ_w(F_p). ∎
   282	
   283	**Lemma HX (hidden exponents; proved).** Let x* ∈ Z^M be primitive, i.e. gcd of its entries is 1, and a function of
   284	the transcript 𝒯. Then B_ := Σ x*_i b_i is uniform on Z/n given 𝒯, so Pr[gcd(B_, n) = 1 | 𝒯] = φ(n)/n ≥
   285	1/(1 + log₂ n).
   286	- If gcd(B_, n) = 1 and A_ := Σ x*_i a_i, then A_ + X·B_ ≡ λ (mod n), so X = (λ − A_)B_^{−1} mod n. This is exact
   287	  for every prime power of n at once, with no factorisation of n.
   288	- *Proof.*
   289	  - By Lemma T_p the b_i are i.i.d. uniform given 𝒯.
   290	  - b ↦ b·x* mod n is a surjective homomorphism, since gcd(x*) = 1 and so a Bezout vector exists. Hence the image of
   291	    the uniform law is uniform.
   292	  - log_g W(x*) = Σ x*_i(a_i + Xb_i) = A_ + X·B_ (mod n), by T_i = g^{a_i}h^{b_i}. ∎
   293	  - The bound φ(n)/n ≥ 1/(1 + log₂ n) is codex dlog_1's elementary telescoping. Mertens gives ≫ 1/log log n.
   294	
   295	**Algorithm EXACT_DEPENDENCE and Proposition XK (proved).** On M ≥ C_col + d rows:
   296	1. Compute E exactly (Lemma XK0).
   297	2. Pick a random prime q ∈ [Q, 2Q] with Q := M·(log p)³. Compute by Gaussian elimination mod q a rank profile
   298	   (I, J), |I| = |J| = ρ.
   299	3. For d rows i ∉ I, solve yᵀE_{I,J} = −E_{i,J} over Q by Dixon's p-adic lifting (Dixon 1982), and clear
   300	   denominators: x_i := D·(y, e_i).
   301	4. Verify x_iᵀE = 0 exactly on all columns. If it fails, then rank_Q E > ρ: restart step 2.
   302	5. Apply Lemma XK4 to s = r_U + 1 of the x_i, giving n ≠ 0.
   303	6. Put x* := Σ n_j x_j / gcd. Then x* ∈ Λ₂ is primitive (Lemma XK3).
   304	7. Compute W(x*), λ (Lemma XK5) and A_, B_ (Lemma HX).
   305	
   306	**Proposition XK.** Steps 1–7 are Las Vegas. They output a primitive x* ∈ Λ₂ and λ with log_g W(x*) = λ in expected
   307	time L^{3a+o(1)} when M = BL^{o(1)} and B = L^a.
   308	
   309	*Proof.*
   310	- **Correctness.** Lemmas XK1–XK5.
   311	- **Step 2.**
   312	  - The ρ_Q × ρ_Q minor of a fixed basis has |det| ≤ ∏‖rows‖ ≤ (O(k log p))^{1.5ρ}. Each row has ≤ 2k(1 + log₂p)
   313	    nonzeros, each O(k log p).
   314	  - So at most O(ρ log log p) primes divide it. [Q, 2Q] contains ≫ M(log p)²/log M ≫ ρ(log p) primes, so
   315	    Pr[rank mod q < rank_Q] ≤ 1/2.
   316	  - A nonsingular minor mod q is nonsingular over Q, so ρ ≤ rank_Q. Step 4 detects ρ < rank_Q.
   317	  - Cost O(M·C_col·ρ) operations mod q = L^{3a+o(1)}.
   318	- **Step 3.**
   319	  - Dixon: one Q-inverse mod a prime (ρ³), then O(ρ log(ρ‖E‖)) lifting steps of O(ρ²) each, for each of d right-hand
   320	    sides. Total Õ(ρ³) = L^{3a+o(1)}.
   321	  - The outputs have β_x = O(ρ log log p) = L^{a+o(1)} bits (Cramer/Hadamard).
   322	- **Steps 4–7.**
   323	  - Step 4: nnz(E)·β_x = L^{2a+o(1)}.
   324	  - Step 5: Lemma XK4 with β_x = L^{a+o(1)} costs L^{2a+o(1)}.
   325	  - Step 7: M modular exponentiations with L^{a+o(1)}-bit exponents cost L^{2a+o(1)}.
   326	  - Lemma XK0(iii): Õ(B) + poly(log p).
   327	- ∎
   328	
   329	Remarks.
   330	- (i) **κ = 3** comes from dense elimination. A sparse rank profile plus sparse rational solving would give smaller
   331	  κ, but rigorous sparse integer solvers (EGGSV 2006) rely on an unproved block-projection conjecture, so we do not
   332	  use them. Only κ ≤ 1 + 1/θ matters for the constants.
   333	- (ii) **Black-box alternative.** Ge / Lenstra–Silverberg Thm 1.11(i) computes generators of the multiplicative
   334	  relation lattice of the explicit row elements in polynomial time with unspecified exponent. This is the route of r19
   335	  §2 and dlog_1 §4. It gives Theorem DL with 3a replaced by Ca, and C unquantified.
   336	- (iii) **Factoring.** The same device gives a character-free replacement for LV6 in Theorem A (also noted in r19 §2).
   337	  - Take Λ₂′ := {x ∈ Λ₂ : γ_x ∈ μ_K², sign product +1}, a sublattice of index ≤ 4.
   338	  - Its reduction mod 2 has dimension ≥ rank Λ₂ − 2, so with M ≥ C_col + d + 2 there is x with some odd x_i.
   339	  - Then (∏r_i^{x_i})² ≡ ψ(ζ′)² with ζ′ ∈ μ_K computable, and DD applies.
   340	  - Cost L^{3a} instead of L^{2a}. That is harmless for θ ≤ 1/2, i.e. whenever c < 1.
   341	  - It would remove Lagarias–Odlyzko, Stark/Landau and the Hensel root from Theorem A.
   342	  - Not worked out further here (lv6's area); labelled **proved (sketch)**.
   343	
   344	## 5. Theorem DL, cost and constants (proved from A)
   345	
   346	**Theorem DL.** Fix admissible (d, k, μ, η₀) with θ < 1. DLOG is Las Vegas and has expected time
   347	  L_p[1/2, c_DL(θ) + o(1)],  c_DL(θ) := min_{a>0} max(a + 1/√2, a + θ/(2a), 3a),
   348	uniformly for primes p ≥ p₀(d, k, μ, η₀). For θ ≤ 1/2, c_DL = (1 + θ)/√2, attained at a = θ/√2.
   349	
   350	*Proof.*
   351	- **Rows.** With B = L^a and the same per-row cost as Theorem A's Step 7 (via §3 and Lemma S_p), C_col + d =
   352	  BL^{o(1)} rows cost L^{a + max(1/√2, θ/(2a)) + o(1)}. Field selection costs L^{max(1/√2, θ/(2a)) + o(1)}.
   353	- **Post-processing** costs L^{3a+o(1)} (Proposition XK).
   354	- **Rounds** are geometric with success ≥ φ(n)/n (Lemma HX), so the expected number is ≤ 1 + log₂ n = L^{o(1)}.
   355	  Fresh rows are drawn each round, so the rounds are independent. The field is kept.
   356	- **Total.** Wald's identity gives L^{max(a + 1/√2, a + θ/(2a), 3a) + o(1)}.
   357	- **Minimisation.** Let f₁ = a + 1/√2, f₂ = a + θ/(2a), f₃ = 3a.
   358	  - At a = θ/√2: f₁ = f₂ = (1 + θ)/√2, and f₃ = 3θ/√2 ≤ (1 + θ)/√2 iff θ ≤ 1/2.
   359	  - min_a max(f₁, f₂) = (1 + θ)/√2 for θ < 1 (thmA Step 10), so no a does better.
   360	  - For θ > 1/2, the minimum is where f₂ = f₃, at a = √θ/2, giving c = 3√θ/2. Check: f₁ = √θ/2 + 1/√2 ≤ 3√θ/2 iff
   361	    θ ≥ 1/2.
   362	  - `src/dlog/params.py` computes all of this exactly with sympy. ∎
   363	
   364	So c_DL(θ) = (1 + θ)/√2 for θ ≤ 1/2, and 3√θ/2 for 1/2 < θ < 1. In every case it is < √2, since 3√θ/2 < √2 iff
   365	θ < 8/9, and the minimal θ at d = 3 is 3/4.
